WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.490
<v Instructor>Now let's get into the API gateway.</v>

2
00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:05.290
So at a high level we know what a API gateway does,

3
00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:07.980
it exposes a REST API to our clients.

4
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.870
Usually it will proxy a request to Lambda, but not only,

5
00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.370
and we'll see this in this lecture.

6
00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:15.810
And Lambda can do some create-read-update-delete

7
00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.360
on a Dynamo DB table, that is some very classic

8
00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.270
solution architecture that is serverless,

9
00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:21.560
but it can do so much more.

10
00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:22.920
But as in the general idea,

11
00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.990
we have our first point of contact with our client

12
00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:28.460
is going to be our Amazon API Gateway.

13
00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:29.293
So what does it do?

14
00:00:29.293 --> 00:00:31.850
It helps expose Lambda functions, but not only,

15
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:36.850
also HTTP endpoints and AWS services as an API.

16
00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:38.650
But why would you leverage an API gateway

17
00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:40.940
instead of just calling these things directly?

18
00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.210
Well, the API gateway gives you a lot of features

19
00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:46.920
that you may want if you are maintaining APIs at scale.

20
00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.380
The first one is API versioning.

21
00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:51.100
So you can publish multiple versions of your API,

22
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.100
which is really helpful to make sure your clients

23
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.370
can migrate from one version to another as you add features.

24
00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:57.590
It gives you authorization.

25
00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.580
So we'll see the integration for security on that.

26
00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:03.500
Traffic management so you can define API keys,

27
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.670
usage plan and throttles.

28
00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:06.930
You can get huge scale.

29
00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:10.020
So the API Gateway does scale very well.

30
00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.020
It's serverless, so you don't you manage any server

31
00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:13.150
for these features.

32
00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.690
You can do some request and response transformations

33
00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.480
directly from the API gateway,

34
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:19.940
for some compatibility issues for example.

35
00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:23.170
You can publish an API as an open API spec,

36
00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:25.470
or import an API as an open API spec.

37
00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:28.540
The idea is that, when you have an open API specification,

38
00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:29.880
your clients can just generate

39
00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:33.010
a client library automatically from that specification

40
00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:34.820
and start using your API on the go,

41
00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:39.710
which is a lot of time saved and has support for CORS.

42
00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:42.090
Okay, so some limits to know right off the bat

43
00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:43.600
for the Amazon API Gateway,

44
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.250
when you think as a solution architect

45
00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:46.890
is it the right thing for me?

46
00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:50.850
Number one is, there is a 29-seconds timeout,

47
00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:52.830
and that is a very important limit.

48
00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:56.140
So if you are proxying a request to a Lambda function,

49
00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:57.010
but that Lambda function,

50
00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:59.710
even though it has a 15 minutes timeout,

51
00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:02.490
takes three minutes to process a request,

52
00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.660
your Amazon API Gateway will timeout

53
00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.120
before your Lambda function,

54
00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.970
because the timeouts on any requests on the API gateway

55
00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:10.930
is 29 seconds.

56
00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.330
So that is a really important limit to know

57
00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:15.720
when you look at your solution architecture.

58
00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.080
The second thing is, the 10 megabytes max payload size,

59
00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:20.470
and I'll be talking about it more.

60
00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:21.303
But the idea is that you cannot send

61
00:02:21.303 --> 00:02:24.610
more than 10 megabytes of data in one request

62
00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:26.510
through the Amazon API Gateway.

63
00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:27.810
So again, if you're trying to upload

64
00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.200
big files onto whatever,

65
00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:32.540
maybe the Amazon API Gateway is not the right thing for you.

66
00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.600
And it's super important to take into account

67
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.593
these two limits when designing your solution architecture.

68
00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.500
Okay, so something we've already seen are deployment stages.

69
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:43.390
So the API changes are deployed to stages

70
00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:45.160
and we can have as many stages as we want,

71
00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.440
and you can name it however you want.

72
00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.670
For example, dev, test, prod, and so on.

73
00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.090
And the stages could be rolled back,

74
00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:54.550
as a history of deployment is kept.

75
00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:56.430
So you can roll back in previous stages

76
00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:57.710
in case a deployment was wrong.

77
00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.690
It's a really neat feature in case you something bad

78
00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:03.220
and want to get back in time.

79
00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:05.610
So we've seen how to deploy a stage

80
00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:06.790
and what it can be pointed to.

81
00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:09.080
So it can be pointed to a prod alias

82
00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:10.730
or a test alias for you Lambda function,

83
00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:12.930
and these aliases can be pointing to V1, V2.

84
00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:14.730
I'm not going to go over it again,

85
00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:17.650
but you remember this from a previous lecture.

86
00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:19.200
Next, for the integrations.

87
00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.350
So we have HTTP-type of integration,

88
00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:24.530
in case you have an HTTP endpoint exposed in your backend,

89
00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.900
for example, you have an internal HTTP API on premise

90
00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:28.370
or you have an application load balancer

91
00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:31.120
and you want to add an API gateway in front of it,

92
00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.160
why would you do it?

93
00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:35.070
Maybe you want to add rate-limiting, caching features,

94
00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:38.220
user authentication, API keys, et cetera, et cetera.

95
00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:39.450
So these could be very good reasons

96
00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:41.950
to add an API gateway as an extra layer.

97
00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:43.610
There are Lambda functions and they're very classic,

98
00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:44.750
we've seen it many times.

99
00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:47.700
So the API gateway will invoke a Lambda function,

100
00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.230
and it's a very easy way to create a REST API

101
00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:53.823
that is backed by AWS Lambda, all in a serverless fashion.

102
00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:55.960
Also very important.

103
00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:57.610
This is something that's very prominent

104
00:03:57.610 --> 00:03:59.110
in the devops exam that still comes up

105
00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:01.030
in the solutions architect professional exam,

106
00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:04.690
which is that you can expose a service specific API

107
00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:06.340
through the API gateway.

108
00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:07.950
So it's any API.

109
00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:10.180
So some very common use cases for example

110
00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:13.090
would be to start a Step Functions workflow

111
00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:15.180
or to post a message to SQS.

112
00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:18.610
But again, leveraging these features of authentication

113
00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:21.300
to deploy publicly, to have rate control and so on.

114
00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.770
So it is very common to have an API gateway

115
00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:27.480
proxying an AWS service for a specific API.

116
00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:30.570
Now let's talk about directly one of these application

117
00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.500
as a solution architecture discussion.

118
00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:35.910
So we want to have API gateway in front of S3

119
00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:37.590
to upload a file.

120
00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:39.160
But the thing, is if you remember what I said

121
00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:40.660
in the very beginning of this lecture,

122
00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:43.450
I said you will be impacted by the 10 megabytes

123
00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:44.600
payload size limits.

124
00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:45.920
So let's have an example.

125
00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:47.360
We have the client application

126
00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.420
and we have an S3 bucket and it's private,

127
00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:52.995
and we want to allow users to upload files into S3.

128
00:04:52.995 --> 00:04:56.150
One way do it, we'd say okay, we have an API gateway

129
00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:59.020
and the API gateway is going to be a proxy

130
00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:03.200
in front of the service S3 for the API put objects.

131
00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:04.940
So we can say okay, I wanna upload a file,

132
00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:07.210
and then the API gateway with the proper configuration

133
00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:09.410
will upload that file into S3.

134
00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:11.550
But the limits here is that you cannot go over

135
00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:13.560
10 megabytes of payload size.

136
00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:15.270
So if you want to upload a big file,

137
00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:16.740
you'll run into some issues.

138
00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:19.200
So this is not a perfect architecture,

139
00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.200
but it is a possible architecture.

140
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:22.610
And this is the kind of things you have to go

141
00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:25.530
and really really look at when you go into the exam.

142
00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:27.570
Now, what is a better architecture?

143
00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:28.920
Well, we have the same kind of things.

144
00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:31.440
The client application does talk to the API gateway,

145
00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:33.680
because we like the features of the API gateway,

146
00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:36.440
and says I want to upload a file.

147
00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:39.567
Now, the API gateway will talk to a Lambda function,

148
00:05:39.567 --> 00:05:41.600
and that Lambda function can do something.

149
00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:45.560
It can generate a pre-signed URL from our S3 buckets.

150
00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:48.620
And what it does by getting this pre-signed URL

151
00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:51.590
is that it will return the URL itself, which is very light,

152
00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:53.210
and the real URL will be forwarded

153
00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:55.200
to our client application from the API gateway.

154
00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:56.900
And then, using that pre-signed URL

155
00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:58.510
directly from our client application,

156
00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:01.600
we are able to upload directly into S3,

157
00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.240
any file of any size we want.

158
00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:06.430
So this architecture is much better.

159
00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:08.130
Yes, there is an extra Lambda function,

160
00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:11.180
but it's scales a lot better, it respects every component

161
00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:12.490
for what it's supposed to be doing,

162
00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:14.600
and using a pre-signed URL, we can upload files

163
00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:18.520
of megabytes, gigabytes or terabytes, whatever we want.

164
00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:21.200
So this is a much better solution architecture.

165
00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:23.420
Again, these two architectures work.

166
00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:25.850
It depends on what kind of limits you want to have.

167
00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:27.030
And again, this is the kind of things

168
00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:28.820
that the exam will test you on.

169
00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:31.600
Now, the API gateway has different endpoint types.

170
00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:32.990
The first one is Edge-Optimized,

171
00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:36.510
which a default which means that your requests

172
00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:39.250
are routed through the CloudFront Edge locations.

173
00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:40.390
So that improves latencies,

174
00:06:40.390 --> 00:06:42.390
and that's really helpful when you have global clients.

175
00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:47.260
But your API gateway itself still lives in only one region.

176
00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:50.560
Then you can deploy it also as a regional API gateway,

177
00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:51.680
so that means that all the clients

178
00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:53.970
are within the same region, otherwise it doesn't make sense,

179
00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:55.727
and you could manually combine this with CloudFront

180
00:06:55.727 --> 00:06:57.610
if you want to have more control

181
00:06:57.610 --> 00:07:00.010
over the caching strategies, distribution,

182
00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:01.000
that kind of things.

183
00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:02.700
So by default Edge optimized,

184
00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:05.390
but you could have a regional API gateway

185
00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:07.520
and combine this with CloudFront.

186
00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:08.610
Finally, private,

187
00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:11.640
where you want to just deploy things only within your VPC

188
00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:14.420
and get access from the ENI,

189
00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:16.090
and you need to use a resource policy

190
00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:18.950
to define access to your private API gateway.

191
00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:20.650
So remember, three different ways

192
00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:22.150
of deploying your API gateway.

193
00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:24.740
Next, super important,

194
00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:28.090
you can cache API responses from your API gateway.

195
00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:29.470
Why would you use caching?

196
00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:32.540
Well, to reduce the number of calls made to the backend.

197
00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:35.390
So the idea is that your client talks to the API gateway,

198
00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:36.223
and your API gateway,

199
00:07:36.223 --> 00:07:38.750
before issuing a request to the backend,

200
00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:40.590
will check the gateway cache,

201
00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:41.930
and if it is available in the cache

202
00:07:41.930 --> 00:07:43.520
it will return it to the clients.

203
00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:46.560
Otherwise, it will do a query in the backend

204
00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:47.840
if there's a cache miss

205
00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:50.420
and store the result in the gateway cache.

206
00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:51.580
This is very helpful.

207
00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:54.580
And then your data in your cache has a time to live,

208
00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:56.630
which is 300 seconds by default.

209
00:07:56.630 --> 00:07:57.930
So five minutes.

210
00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:00.310
But you can set it to zero if you don't wanna cache,

211
00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:02.650
or one hour if you want your data

212
00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:04.750
to be cached for a long time.

213
00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:07.070
Caches are defined per stage,

214
00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:11.070
and it's possible to override each cache setting per method.

215
00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:12.950
So if you have a get on whatever URL,

216
00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:14.040
or a put in whatever URL,

217
00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:15.820
and you don't want it to cache for that thing,

218
00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:17.100
you can overwrite the cache setting

219
00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:19.120
for these specific methods.

220
00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:22.240
The clients themselves as well can invalidate the cache

221
00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:25.630
using a header called "Cache-Control: max-age=0,"

222
00:08:25.630 --> 00:08:27.740
and you need the proper IM authorization to do this,

223
00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:28.700
but it's possible for the client

224
00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:31.010
to basically bypass the cache.

225
00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:33.540
You also have the ability to flush the entire cache,

226
00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:36.560
so it means invalidating it immediately,

227
00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:39.950
if you wanted to repopulate the cache at one point of time.

228
00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:41.840
Finally, the cache can be encrypted,

229
00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:43.306
and you can define a capacity of the cache

230
00:08:43.306 --> 00:08:47.290
between 0.5 gigabytes to 237 gigabyte.

231
00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:49.290
So it can be quite big.

232
00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:50.440
Now in terms of errors,

233
00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:52.680
it's very important to understand the API gateway errors

234
00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:55.720
because they indicate to you a lot of information.

235
00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:59.640
In case you get a 4xx, that means it's client errors.

236
00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:02.348
So examples, 400 is Bad Request.

237
00:09:02.348 --> 00:09:04.210
403 is Access Denied,

238
00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:06.190
or maybe your web application firewall

239
00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:07.260
has filtered the request.

240
00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:10.800
429 is quota exceeded, or a throttle

241
00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:13.000
directly coming from the API gateway.

242
00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:15.380
And when you get a 5xx,

243
00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:17.430
that means it's a server-side error.

244
00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:19.000
So some notable one.

245
00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:21.730
502 means a Bad Gateway Exception.

246
00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:23.250
That means usually when the Lambda function

247
00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.790
returns a bad output, or occasionally

248
00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:28.770
when you have out of order invocations due to heavy loads.

249
00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:31.700
You have 503, which is Service Unavailable Exception.

250
00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:33.680
So this is when the backend cannot respond.

251
00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:36.230
And 504, which is Integration Failure.

252
00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:37.590
And one example is the limit

253
00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:40.430
I was talking to you about in the beginning of this lecture,

254
00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:43.110
which is for example when the endpoint request timed out

255
00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:45.680
because the API gateway has a request timeout

256
00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:48.160
of about 29 seconds maximum.

257
00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:49.910
So this is when you will get a 504.

258
00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:52.690
Next for security.

259
00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:55.910
So you can load SSL certificates onto your API gateway,

260
00:09:55.910 --> 00:09:58.870
and then you use Route53 to define a CNAME.

261
00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:00.020
You can have resource policy,

262
00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:01.820
for example, like an S3 bucket policy.

263
00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:04.290
So this controls who can access the API,

264
00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:06.780
which accounts, which IP, which CIDR blocks,

265
00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:08.840
which VPC or VPC endpoints.

266
00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:10.170
And then, IAM Execution Roles

267
00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:11.520
for the API gateway at the API level,

268
00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:13.180
for example, if your AIP gateway

269
00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:14.490
needs to invoke a Lambda Functions

270
00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:16.640
and you need to define the IAM roles for that.

271
00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:19.610
It support CORS, so Cross-origin Resource Sharing.

272
00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:22.670
That means it gives you some browser-based security

273
00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:25.290
and it controls which domain can call your API,

274
00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:27.203
if the client is well behaved.

275
00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:30.400
Authentication, there's three ways.

276
00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:32.030
You have IAM-based access.

277
00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:33.600
This is good when you want to provide access

278
00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:34.830
within your own infrastructure,

279
00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:38.130
and it uses the IAM credentials passed in in the header

280
00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:40.660
through something called Sig V4.

281
00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:42.490
You have Lambda-based authorizers,

282
00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:44.550
used to be known as custom authorizers,

283
00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:46.550
and this is when you want to use a Lambda function

284
00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:48.950
to verify a custom authentication token,

285
00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:51.760
things like Oauth, SAML or 3rd party authentication,

286
00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:54.110
and you have to program the Lambda function

287
00:10:54.110 --> 00:10:56.530
to verify the authentication mechanism.

288
00:10:56.530 --> 00:11:00.170
Or we can use the deep integration with Cognito User Pool,

289
00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:03.641
in which case the client has to authenticate with Cognito

290
00:11:03.641 --> 00:11:04.474
and the client will pass in

291
00:11:04.474 --> 00:11:07.210
the authentication code token to API gateway,

292
00:11:07.210 --> 00:11:09.170
and the API gateway knows out of the box

293
00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:11.370
how to verify that token.

294
00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:12.600
So what does it look like?

295
00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:15.210
With the client connecting to the Cognito User Pool,

296
00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:17.710
authenticating and then gets a token back,

297
00:11:17.710 --> 00:11:20.280
it passes the token to the API gateway

298
00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:22.650
and the API gateway can verify that token

299
00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:24.920
with the Cognito User Pool.

300
00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:28.500
And then if it does, then it can invoke your Lambda function

301
00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:30.480
and it will pass the identity of the color

302
00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:31.313
to the Lambda function,

303
00:11:31.313 --> 00:11:35.410
so you can do some extra logic on your Lambda function side.

304
00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:38.020
So the thing to notice here is that your client

305
00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:40.880
has to first authenticate with Cognito User Pool,

306
00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:44.730
and you need to program obviously for this, the right code.

307
00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:46.910
Finally, in terms of logging, monitoring

308
00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:48.320
and tracing, that's very important,

309
00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:51.420
CloudWatch Logs does give you logging at the stage level

310
00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:53.620
for log-level error or info,

311
00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:56.430
and you can log full requests and responses data.

312
00:11:56.430 --> 00:11:58.950
So it can be quite helpful when you want to troubleshoot.

313
00:11:58.950 --> 00:12:01.870
You can also send API gateway access logs

314
00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:02.950
which is customizable,

315
00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:05.420
and the logs can be sent directly if you wanted to

316
00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:06.920
into Kinesis Data Firehose

317
00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:09.470
as an alternative to CloudWatch Logs.

318
00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:10.720
You have CloudWatch Metrics

319
00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:11.930
where you get metrics by stage

320
00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:14.030
and you can enable detailed metrics,

321
00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:15.770
and you get some information such as

322
00:12:15.770 --> 00:12:18.410
the integration latency, the latency overall,

323
00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:20.960
the cache hit count and the cache miss count.

324
00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:22.920
All of these are very important to understand

325
00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:25.860
if you have optimized your API gateway the right way.

326
00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:27.900
Finally, X-Ray will give you tracing

327
00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:29.750
to get extra information about the requests

328
00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:31.100
going through API gateway,

329
00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:33.300
such as latency, errors and so on.

330
00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:36.030
And so, if you have X-Ray on the API gateway

331
00:12:36.030 --> 00:12:37.820
plus X-Ray on your Lambda function,

332
00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:39.570
that will give you the entire picture

333
00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:41.840
of how your architecture is working.

334
00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:43.810
Okay, so lots of good information.

335
00:12:43.810 --> 00:12:44.760
I hope you liked this lecture

336
00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:46.710
and I will see you in the next lecture.

